# docs/app-identity-plan.md. THE READ IS OF THE ARTIFACT, NOT THE MODEL:
# every platform draws SOMETHING where an app icon goes, so
# `expect_app_icon` reads the four quadrant CENTRES off the picture the
# platform ended up holding (WM_GETICON on the real HWND, on Windows).
# The mark is VENDORED to be unreachable by any platform default, and
# tools/check-app-identity.py decodes this frozen line against its
# actual pixels — its N7 self-test counts THREE mentions of the verb in
# this file, so this sentence is load-bearing.
expect label#0 "identity"
expect_app_icon "E01B24/33D17A/1C71D8/F6D32D"
# THE PROMOTION IS THE SECOND WINDOWS SINK: a custom caption is minted
# by a promotion and replaces the system one, taking the system-drawn
# icon with it, so `TitleBar.IconSource` puts the mark back. Reading
# that property would be an echo and is refused (app-identity-plan I8);
# this line says only that the arm was reached.
expect_toolbar
expect_toolbar_item "Save" "done"
# A declared name never OVERRIDES a window's own title, it fills the
# blank — which is why the two titles here differ.
expect_title "identity"
expect_title window#1 "Aurora Notes"
set_text entry#0 "hi"
click button#0
expect label#1 "clicked hi"
# THE SECOND READ IS NOT REDUNDANT: a lowering that re-applied on a
# later pass, or a window that took the icon away when it took the
# focus, fails here and nowhere else.
expect_app_icon "E01B24/33D17A/1C71D8/F6D32D"
